(() => {
  const style = document.createElement('style');
  style.textContent = `
    .article-long{max-width:860px;margin:0 auto}.article-long .article-meta{color:var(--blue);font-size:.82rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase}.article-long h1{font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4.5rem);letter-spacing:-.06em;line-height:.98;margin:12px 0 18px}.article-long h2{font-size:1.55rem;letter-spacing:-.035em;margin:38px 0 10px}.article-long p,.article-long li{color:#42516d;font-size:1.05rem}.article-long li{margin:8px 0}.article-long .article-callout{background:#e9efff;border-left:4px solid var(--blue);border-radius:10px;padding:18px 20px;margin:25px 0}.article-long .article-callout p{margin:0}.faq-page{max-width:920px;margin:0 auto}.faq-page h1{font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4.2rem);letter-spacing:-.06em;line-height:1;margin:10px 0 16px}.faq-page details{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:19px;margin:11px 0}.faq-page summary{font-weight:800;cursor:pointer}.faq-page p{color:var(--muted);margin:14px 0 0}.faq-note{background:#07132b;color:#d8e4ff;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;margin-top:25px}.faq-note strong{color:#fff}
  `;
  document.head.append(style);

  const article = `
    <section><article class="article-long">
      <div class="article-meta">SEO Articles · Research-first guide</div>
      <h1>How to use a PikoBuy product link with better research before you buy</h1>
      <p>Most poor shopping decisions are made before a parcel ever reaches a warehouse. A listing may look promising, a title may sound precise, and a price may feel like a shortcut—but none of those things tells you whether the size makes sense, whether the product clue is relevant, or whether the eventual parcel will still be worth shipping. This is why a useful PikoBuy workflow starts with research rather than urgency.</p>
      <p>PikoBuy publicly presents itself as a China shopping and forwarding agent for products from marketplaces including Taobao, Weidian and 1688. Its public homepage says shoppers can search directly, submit a product link, submit an image, or send a sourcing request. It also describes a process in which items are purchased on the shopper’s behalf, received in a China warehouse, inspected and repacked, then shipped using an option selected by the shopper. That is a helpful process map. It is not a promise that every listing is good, every item is available, or every shipping outcome will be the same.</p>
      <p>The practical question, then, is not “Can I open this link?” It is “What information should I collect before I move this link forward?” This guide explains a research-first way to work with product links, photographs, category clues and shipping context. It is an independent guide for discovery. It does not process orders, take payments, handle returns or replace the information shown in a live PikoBuy account.</p>

      <h2>Start with a product clue, not a product claim</h2>
      <p>A product link is an address, not evidence. Before you add it to a buying workflow, write down the basic clue that led you to it: the category, material, shape, stated size range, colour, seller name if visible, and the main reason you saved it. This takes less than a minute and prevents a common mistake: returning to a long list of links later and having no idea why one of them mattered.</p>
      <p>Use the clue to ask a narrower question. For shoes, that might be whether the silhouette, outsole pattern and size information are consistent. For a hoodie, it may be fabric weight, fit language and measurement context. For a bag, dimensions, hardware and interior layout matter more than a dramatic product photo. A good research note does not say “looks good.” It says what you still need to check.</p>
      <p>PikoBuy’s public workflow allows more than a pasted link: shoppers can also search, submit images or make sourcing requests. That flexibility is useful when a link is incomplete or when the item is only a visual reference. But it should not be mistaken for verification. A sourcing request can help move a search forward; it cannot turn an unclear reference into a guaranteed match. Keep the request specific: describe the item, the material or feature that matters, the acceptable colour and the measurement question you need answered.</p>

      <h2>Read the listing as a set of signals</h2>
      <p>Many people overvalue the title because it is the fastest thing to read. Titles on marketplace listings can be long, translated oddly, generic or written to include many search phrases. Treat them as one signal among several. Compare the title with the visible photos, options, measurements and any product-specific details. If the title says one thing but the photos or options imply another, flag that mismatch before moving on.</p>
      <p>Photos are useful for visible features, not for invisible promises. They can help you compare shape, print placement, seams, colour blocking, hardware, labels and included parts. They cannot establish durability, seller conduct, future stock, authenticity, delivery speed or customs treatment. The same boundary applies to community comments and spreadsheets: they can be excellent discovery tools, but they are not guarantees.</p>
      <p>A simple three-column note works well. In the first column, record what the listing visibly shows. In the second, record what the seller states, such as a size chart or material description. In the third, record the uncertainty that remains. That third column is the most valuable one. It tells you whether you need another angle, a clearer measurement, a different listing or a decision to skip the item.</p>

      <h2>Use category-specific checks before you commit</h2>
      <p>Different product types fail in different ways. A single generic checklist is better than no checklist, but it is not enough for serious comparison. Shoes deserve checks around toe shape, heel shape, outsole, size label and overall proportions. Hoodies and T-shirts deserve checks around chest measurement, length, sleeve proportion, fabric description and print placement. Jackets need attention to lining, closure hardware, collar structure and outer-material details. Pants and shorts should be compared through waist, rise, inseam, hem and fabric weight rather than a single letter size.</p>
      <p>Accessories require an equally specific approach. For a bag, record dimensions, strap length, closure type, hardware finish and the photos that show the inside. For a watch, check the case, dial, strap, clasp and any close-up details you actually care about. For electronics, keep the question practical: specifications, plugs, language, compatibility and what is included. If those details are unclear, no attractive photo can solve the problem.</p>
      <div class="article-callout"><p><strong>Research rule:</strong> do not ask a warehouse photo to answer a question that the listing itself has not defined. First decide what you are comparing; then look for the evidence that can actually answer it.</p></div>

      <h2>What warehouse inspection can—and cannot—do</h2>
      <p>PikoBuy says publicly that items undergo quality inspection and repacking after arriving at its China warehouse. That is useful because it means there is a warehouse stage between seller dispatch and international shipping. It also gives shoppers a point at which visible item details and packaging context can be reviewed. In practice, this is where a clear pre-purchase note pays off: you already know which visible features, measurements or accessories matter to your decision.</p>
      <p>Still, “inspection” should not be rewritten into a guarantee. An inspection process does not make an item universally high quality. It does not guarantee that a seller’s description was complete, that colour will look identical on every screen, that a product will fit, or that an item will meet a personal standard that was never stated. It is best understood as a chance to examine available evidence before the international leg, not as a substitute for judgment.</p>
      <p>Repacking is also part of PikoBuy’s public description. Repacking can matter to parcel planning because packaging affects how a parcel is prepared. But shoppers should avoid inventing a universal saving, a fixed final weight or a standard delivery time. Those outcomes depend on the actual items, the packaging used and the shipping option available at the time of submission.</p>

      <h2>Treat shipping as part of the decision, not the final afterthought</h2>
      <p>PikoBuy’s public homepage says users choose the shipping option that suits them and that items are delivered from its China warehouse. This makes shipping a real choice, not a background detail. The sensible time to think about that choice is before you build a large collection of unrelated products.</p>
      <p>Start by grouping items according to what makes them difficult to ship: bulky footwear, dense hardware, fragile components, thick outerwear or electronics. Then ask whether the item is still useful if shipping and packaging are considered alongside the product price. You do not need to guess a universal rate to make a better decision. You only need to recognise that physical volume, packaging and the chosen line can change the total.</p>
      <p>Do not publish or rely on invented delivery promises. Routes, local rules, carrier operations and customs processes are outside a discovery guide’s control. The responsible approach is to use current options and estimates in the live service when they are available, then choose based on your own destination, budget and tolerance for uncertainty.</p>

      <h2>A calm final checklist</h2>
      <p>Before you continue from a research list to a live product search, pause for five questions. Do I know exactly which variation I want? Have I checked the evidence that matters for this category? Is the size or measurement context clear enough? What uncertainty remains after the listing and images? And would I still keep this item on my shortlist if parcel planning changes the total?</p>
      <p>If the answer to one of those questions is no, you do not need to force a purchase decision. Save the clue, improve the note and compare another option. The strongest use of a product-link workflow is not speed. It is the ability to make each next step more informed than the last.</p>
      <p>This is the role of an independent research guide: help you narrow the search, identify the right questions and keep discovery separate from payment, account support and order handling. When you are ready to continue searching, use the FindSpreadsheet database with a precise category or product clue. For live order, payment, warehouse, shipping or support decisions, rely on the current information inside your own PikoBuy account.</p>
    </article></section>`;

  const faq = `
    <section><div class="faq-page">
      <div class="kicker">Fact-based FAQ</div>
      <h1>PikoBuy research questions, answered without invented promises.</h1>
      <details open><summary>Is this an official PikoBuy website?</summary><p>No. This is an independent research guide. It does not sell items, take payments, place orders, ship parcels or access customer accounts.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What buying methods does PikoBuy publicly describe?</summary><p>Its public homepage says shoppers can search directly or submit product links, images or sourcing requests. It also says it can help confirm product details and proceed with the purchase process.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Which marketplaces does PikoBuy mention?</summary><p>The public homepage describes PikoBuy as an agent for shopping from Taobao, Weidian and 1688. Availability of a specific listing still needs to be checked in the live workflow.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What happens after an item arrives at the warehouse?</summary><p>PikoBuy publicly says items undergo quality inspection and repacking at its China warehouse. This is a warehouse stage for reviewing available evidence and preparing items; it is not a blanket guarantee of quality, fit, authenticity or seller behaviour.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can QC photographs guarantee that an item is good?</summary><p>No. Photos can help assess visible details such as shape, colour, measurements and included parts. They cannot guarantee future stock, durability, fit, customs outcomes, delivery time or a seller’s behaviour.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does PikoBuy ship worldwide?</summary><p>Its public homepage says it ships worldwide and lets users choose a shipping option. Actual service, route choices, fees and destination requirements can change, so confirm those details in the live service before paying.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Why should I think about shipping before choosing products?</summary><p>Items differ in weight, volume, packaging and handling needs. The final parcel and shipping option are part of the total decision, so a low item price alone is not enough context.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Why does this guide send searches to FindSpreadsheet?</summary><p>FindSpreadsheet is the product-discovery database used by this guide. It helps you continue with a precise category or product clue; it is not a PikoBuy checkout or account-support channel.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Who should answer live order, payment, refund or tracking questions?</summary><p>Use the service connected to your own order and account. This independent guide cannot view orders, confirm account status, quote live shipping or decide refund outcomes.</p></details>
      <div class="faq-note"><strong>Editorial boundary:</strong> these answers distinguish PikoBuy’s publicly described process from outcomes nobody can guarantee. Check live information before you make a payment or submit a parcel.</div>
    </div></section>`;

  const articlePage = document.querySelector('[data-page="articles"]');
  const faqPage = document.querySelector('[data-page="faq"]');
  if (articlePage) articlePage.innerHTML = article;
  if (faqPage) faqPage.innerHTML = faq;
})();
